                                                                            • Confidence
                                                                                                                       Ñ
                                                                                                                 𝑁 (𝜑𝑖 𝑚 𝑐 ) 𝑆 𝜑𝑖 →𝑚 𝑐
                                                                                                      𝐶𝜑𝑖 →𝑚 𝑐 =            =                                        (10)
                                                                                                                    𝑁 (𝜑𝑖 )     𝑆 𝜑𝑖

                                                                 𝑁 (𝜑𝑖 ) represents the number of transactions that include 𝜑𝑖 . The confidence of the rule can be
                                                             interpreted as a conditional probability; a higher confidence level indicates a stronger reliability of the
                                                             rule. In general, the minimum confidence threshold should be established at a fairly high level.

                                                                            • Lift
                                                                                                                   Ñ    
                                                                                                             𝑁 (𝜑𝑖 𝑚 𝑐 ) 𝑁 (𝑚 𝑐 ) 𝐶𝜑𝑖 →𝑚 𝑐
                                                                                                 𝐿 𝜑𝑖 →𝑚 𝑐 =                     =                                   (11)
                                                                                                                𝑁 (𝜑𝑖 )    𝑁       𝑆𝑚𝑐

                                                                 The lift of a rule is defined as the ratio of the rule’s confidence to the support of the consequent.
                                                             This metric illustrates the extent to which the antecedent influences the consequent in comparison
                                                             to the overall context. Consequently, when 𝐿 𝜑𝑖 →𝑚 𝑐 > 1 holds true, the antecedent exerts a positive
                                                             influence on the consequent, with a greater lift indicating a stronger degree of positive influence.
                                                             Conversely, if 𝐿 𝜑𝑖 →𝑚 𝑐 < 1 is true, even with high support and confidence, the rule still not be deemed
                                                             effective.

                                                                            • Deployment Capability

                                                                                                          𝐷 𝜑𝑖 →𝑚 𝑐 = 𝑆 𝜑𝑖 − 𝑆 𝜑𝑖 →𝑚 𝑐                               (12)

                                                                 Deployment capability measures the proportion of instances where the antecedent is true but the
                                                             consequent is not. A higher deployment capability suggests greater potential for improvement in the
                                                             rule; therefore, a lower deployment capability is generally preferred.
                                                                 In conclusion, we have conducted a validity analysis of the rule outcomes from the preceding
                                                             section, and the findings indicate that all our rules are valid and of considerable quality.




